30. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': 'ed6cd9f4-45c4-42a4-b3d1-726f43af46be', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Increased perceived stress is thought to activate the body's adaptive mechanisms, which in turn suppress proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6 to protect tissues from excessive inflammation. Therefore, higher levels of perceived stress would be expected to decrease proinflammatory processes, resulting in a negative association between stress and these cytokines.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "Increased perceived stress is thought to activate the body's adaptive mechanisms, which in turn suppress proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6 to protect tissues from excessive inflammation. Therefore, higher levels of perceived stress would be expected to decrease proinflammatory processes, resulting in a negative association between stress and these cytokines.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

