   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Higher perceived stress increases the likelihood and severity of depressive symptoms, while lower perceived stress reduces them. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where chronic stress directly impacts mood, cognition, and neurobiology, leading to depression. The effect is direct and not fully mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'ed6cd9f4-45c4-42a4-b3d1-726f43af46be', 'prob_std': 0.26220103386735066, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7897868/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165032719324541', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6541026/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7266529535983027, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of perceived stress initiate a physiological stress response, which leads to increased production of cortisol. This heightened cortisol directly suppresses the neural circuits responsible for processing positive emotions, resulting in an immediate onset of depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Therefore, changes in perceived stress (PSS) are rapidly translated into proportional changes in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), regardless of individual coping strategies or social support, through this direct neuroendocrine pathway.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of perceived stress initiate a physiological stress response, which leads to increased production of cortisol. This heightened cortisol directly suppresses the neural circuits responsible for processing positive emotions, resulting in an immediate onset of depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Therefore, changes in perceived stress (PSS) are rapidly translated into proportional changes in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), regardless of individual coping strategies or social support, through this direct neuroendocrine pathway.', 'perplexity': 1.3714306018872102, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8053395169474274, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.032386946332494365, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035489276050134837, 'prob_variance': 0.06874938216110758, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10942315550114, 'avg_nll': 0.3158544299601387}

