39. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can increase physiological and psychological arousal, making it harder to fall or stay asleep, thus worsening sleep disturbance. Conversely, reducing perceived stress directly improves sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance. This relationship is well-established in scientific literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': 'ed6cd9f4-45c4-42a4-b3d1-726f43af46be', 'prob_std': 0.26221817766029076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045300/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431234/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7384388609864314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals who experience higher levels of stress tend to allocate more time to cognitively processing daily events, which directly increases the duration of sleep needed for memory consolidation. As a result, this extended need for sleep often exceeds the typical sleep period, causing subjective reports of sleep disturbance on the PSQI. Therefore, elevated perceived stress (PSS) increases the requirement for sleep, which, when unmet, is reported as greater sleep disturbance (PSQI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals who experience higher levels of stress tend to allocate more time to cognitively processing daily events, which directly increases the duration of sleep needed for memory consolidation. As a result, this extended need for sleep often exceeds the typical sleep period, causing subjective reports of sleep disturbance on the PSQI. Therefore, elevated perceived stress (PSS) increases the requirement for sleep, which, when unmet, is reported as greater sleep disturbance (PSQI).', 'perplexity': 1.376067850533372, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8136113344877486, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02180117614694403, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003922008611347438, 'prob_variance': 0.06875837269548382, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10500313383009, 'avg_nll': 0.3192300482761032}

