43. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination, defined as repetitive and persistent thinking about distressing experiences or feelings, directly heightens depressive symptoms by reinforcing negative thought patterns. Conversely, reducing rumination leads to a decrease in depressive symptoms. This relationship is well-supported in psychological research and does not require mediation by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'ed6cd9f4-45c4-42a4-b3d1-726f43af46be', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Rumination actually helps individuals process their thoughts and emotions, leading to a reduction in depressive symptoms. By repeatedly reflecting on their experiences, people are able to gain insights and resolve negative feelings, which decreases the likelihood of developing or maintaining depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination actually helps individuals process their thoughts and emotions, leading to a reduction in depressive symptoms. By repeatedly reflecting on their experiences, people are able to gain insights and resolve negative feelings, which decreases the likelihood of developing or maintaining depressive symptoms.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

