   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through sustained psychological stress, which activates physiological stress pathways and elevates inflammatory biomarkers. Increased rumination prolongs stress responses, leading to higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing rumination would decrease these proinflammatory processes. This causal link is supported by psychoneuroimmunology research on stress and inflammation.', 'session_id': 'ed6cd9f4-45c4-42a4-b3d1-726f43af46be', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves reflective thinking which can serve as a coping mechanism to reduce psychological stress. This reduction in stress is believed to subsequently lower proinflammatory processes, such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, by promoting emotional regulation and resilience. Therefore, increased rumination should be associated with a decrease in proinflammatory biomarkers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves reflective thinking which can serve as a coping mechanism to reduce psychological stress. This reduction in stress is believed to subsequently lower proinflammatory processes, such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, by promoting emotional regulation and resilience. Therefore, increased rumination should be associated with a decrease in proinflammatory biomarkers.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

