   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) through well-established psychological and physiological mechanisms, such as increased rumination, negative affect, and disruptions in circadian rhythm. An increase in depressive symptoms directly leads to an increase in sleep disturbance, while a decrease in depressive symptoms directly reduces sleep disturbance. This effect is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list. The causal direction is from depressive symptoms to sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': 'ed6cd9f4-45c4-42a4-b3d1-726f43af46be', 'prob_std': 0.2847180015468549, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7381800/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2612129/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6433686/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7576729357061399, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals experiencing heightened depressive symptoms often spend more time in bed attempting to rest, which leads to prolonged periods of inactivity and, consequently, disrupts the body’s natural need for sleep. This extended restfulness during the day diminishes overall sleep drive, resulting in greater difficulty falling and staying asleep at night as measured by the PSQI. Thus, fluctuations in depressive symptoms directly dictate the degree of sleep disturbance, independent of other behavioral or physiological factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals experiencing heightened depressive symptoms often spend more time in bed attempting to rest, which leads to prolonged periods of inactivity and, consequently, disrupts the body’s natural need for sleep. This extended restfulne